# NOTE: .deploy-stamp must NOT be listed here. `railway up` respects .gitignore, so
# ignoring the stamp would strip it from the upload — and the stamp is the ONLY thing
# guaranteeing the upload differs when code moved, which is what forces Railway to
# rebuild instead of skipping. It stays untracked-but-not-ignored on purpose:
# untracked files ARE uploaded, committed ones would churn the diff every deploy.
#
# ⚑ 18 Sep (XC-11) — THE SAME APPLIES TO apps/website/build-identity.txt, and for the same
# two reasons. `ship.sh` writes the short deployed sha into it so the post-deploy read can
# confirm what the website is actually serving; ignoring it would strip it from the upload and
# the read would poll for a file that was never shipped, for ever. Committing it would churn
# the diff every deploy. Untracked-but-not-ignored, exactly like the stamp.
#
# 🛑 IT IS NOT A DOTFILE, DELIBERATELY: `express.static` ignores dotfiles, so `.deploy-stamp`
# is unreachable over HTTP and the locked catch-all answers index.html at 200 instead. That is
# why the website's build identity needed an ordinary filename. `website-freeze.test.ts`
# excludes it by NAME (founder-approved under #605); nothing else about the freeze is relaxed.

# generated byproduct of scripts/build-core-map.py (the manifest itself IS committed)
scripts/.core-trace.json

# ⚑ 18 Sep (Batch 1b) — the REAL PostgREST binary the full-stack harness fetches. 20 MB, and
# version-pinned by scripts/fullstack/fetch-postgrest.sh (with a sha256), so the repo records
# WHICH binary without carrying it. `railway up` never needs it: the harness is local-only.
scripts/fullstack/bin/
